Subject: [PATCH] KVM: arm64: map guest memory executable on first fault

Without FEAT_DIC, KVM maps guest memory non-executable and makes a page
executable only when the guest first executes from it. That permission
fault relaxes the stage-2 entry and invalidates the TLB with TLBI IPAS2E1
followed by TLBI VMALLE1.

When this kernel is itself a guest hypervisor under Apple's
Hypervisor.framework, those invalidations are emulated by discarding
every stage-2 mapping of the nested VM. The nested VM then faults its
whole working set back in, and a workload that keeps executing from new
pages, such as a JIT, runs at a small fraction of its normal speed.

Map normal memory executable on the first fault, as KVM already does
with FEAT_DIC. This stays coherent without DIC because the stage-2 map
walker invalidates the instruction cache before it installs an
executable entry. The cost is instruction cache maintenance on pages
that are never executed.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c b/arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c
--- a/arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c
@@ -1642,8 +1642,15 @@ static int user_mem_abort(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu, phys_addr_t fault_ipa,
 			prot |= KVM_PGTABLE_PROT_NORMAL_NC;
 		else
 			prot |= KVM_PGTABLE_PROT_DEVICE;
-	} else if (cpus_have_final_cap(ARM64_HAS_CACHE_DIC) &&
-		   (!nested || kvm_s2_trans_executable(nested))) {
+	} else if (!nested || kvm_s2_trans_executable(nested)) {
+		/*
+		 * libkrunfw: map normal memory executable on the first fault,
+		 * as if the CPU had DIC. The stage-2 map walker invalidates the
+		 * I-cache before installing an executable PTE, so this stays
+		 * coherent without DIC. It avoids a later permission fault and
+		 * its TLBI IPAS2E1 + TLBI VMALLE1, which Hypervisor.framework
+		 * emulates by dropping every nested stage-2 mapping.
+		 */
 		prot |= KVM_PGTABLE_PROT_X;
 	}
 
